{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>Then Moses sang</b> — This is the language of our Master Solomon [Ibn Gabirol]: when he saw the miracle, it arose in his heart that he would sing a song, and so he did: \"and they spoke, saying,\" etc. And similarly, \"Then Joshua spoke\" (Joshua 10:12) — when he saw the miracle, his heart told him to speak, and so he did: \"and he said before the eyes of Israel.\" And so too the Song of the Well, which begins \"Then Israel sang\" (Numbers 21:17), and after it he explained, \"Spring up, O well, sing to it.\" And similarly, \"And a house he made for Pharaoh's daughter\" (I Kings 7:8) — he planned in his heart to make her the house. \"Then Solomon built\" (I Kings 11:7) — the Sages of Israel explained it (Sanhedrin 91a) that his heart sought to build but he did not build. This is to establish the plain sense.\n\nAnd what will the Rav say about the verse \"They made a calf at Horeb\" (Psalms 106:19), \"How often they rebelled against Him in the wilderness, they grieved Him in the desert\" (Psalms 78:40), and the entire psalm likewise: \"He killed their vines with hail\" (Psalms 78:47), \"He sent among them swarms of flies\" (Psalms 78:45), and similarly \"From where do they come to you?\" (II Kings 20:14), \"From the strikings with which the Arameans strike him\" (II Kings 8:29, 9:15), and similarly \"Moses took the tent\" (Exodus 33:7) — for it is not a participle form, since he took it only once. Rather, the way of the language is to put the future in place of the past, and likewise they say the opposite in many places. And the reason is that it is the custom of language that the one recounting a matter places himself at whatever time he wishes, and alludes to the event from within it. Sometimes he places himself at the time of the event and speaks of it in the present tense, standing at its beginning, and says \"Israel sang\" as though they are singing before him, and so all of them. And sometimes he delays himself and says: this was already done — and all to convey the matter accurately. Therefore, most instances where this occurs will be in the context of prophecies." "<b>For He is highly exalted</b> — at the head of the songs, and for everything with which I praise Him, there is still more addition to be found in Him. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Exodus 15:1). He made it an expression of exaltation and greatness. And perhaps that is so, and like it: \"and here He sets a bound to the pride of your waves\" (Job 38:11), \"for the waters swelled\" (Ezekiel 47:5), \"and He will swell like the lion He hunts me\" (Job 10:16) — an expression of greatness and increase. But the correct view is that of Onkelos (Onkelos on Exodus 15:1): an expression of actual haughtiness, for He exalted Himself over the horse that prides itself in battle and over the mighty warrior riding it, for He hurled them both into the sea. And similarly, \"and in the greatness of Your excellence\" (Exodus 15:7) — and so all of them are expressions of haughtiness, for the one who exalts himself raises himself in superiority."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {0 "Ramban quotes Ibn Ezra's grammatical argument by the epithet 'the Rav' (Maimonides) in the middle paragraph; the reference is to Ibn Ezra's discussion of the yiqtol-consecutive forms cited from Psalms and Kings."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:08:42.983533848Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}